-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/invest/strategies/fix-and-flip/#faq", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Does BrickWood finance new construction for investors?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. BrickWood Mortgage finances ground-up construction for investors, in most cases without income documents. The loan can stand on the deal when the finished house cash flows as a rental or the numbers show it selling for at least 10 percent over the build cost. Primary-home construction loans take longer and involve more paperwork."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I do a 1031 exchange on a flip?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Usually no. A house bought to fix and resell is inventory, not investment property, and the IRS excludes property held for sale from 1031 treatment. If the exchange option matters to you, hold the finished house as a rental first and confirm the timing with a tax advisor."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is fix and flip profitable in Myrtle Beach?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It can be, but the margins are thinner than the television version. Demand here is seasonal, so exit timing matters. Carrying costs, the higher investment tax rate, and selling costs all eat into profit. Your profit comes down to the price you pay and the accuracy of the rehab budget."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How do I calculate flip profit?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Start with the ARV, what the house will be worth once it is fixed up. Subtract the purchase price, the rehab cost, every holding cost, and the selling costs. What remains is profit. Underestimating holding and selling costs is the most common mistake."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What financing do flippers use?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Most flips use a hard money loan, a short-term loan that closes quickly and can pay for part of the repairs. The rate is higher than a normal mortgage, so the faster you finish and sell, the more you keep."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What if my flip does not sell?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Always model a rental backup. If the resale market softens, refinance into a DSCR loan, which qualifies on the property's rent, and hold the house as a rental. That converts the flip into a BRRRR and gives the deal a floor. Our long-term rental analyzer shows whether it would cash flow."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "When is the best time to sell a flip on the Grand Strand?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Resale demand is generally strongest heading into the spring and summer buying season. A flip that finishes in the slower months can sit. Time the completion to the selling season to protect the margin."}}]}</script>
