-Where the deals come from
+## How do we find a house worth flipping?
+Our agents use a deal finder we built to look at every home for sale on the Grand Strand in one pass. An agent checks everything it finds:
 
-## Wholesaling is mostly closed in South Carolina
-South Carolina's 2024 license law treats marketing a home you only hold under contract as wholesaling. Licensed brokerages are barred from doing it or assisting it, and without a license a wholesaler may market only the contract itself, never the property.
-The discounted houses that used to move through wholesalers now reach buyers through agents, estate sales and bank-owned listings. Who hears about one first comes down to who knows the seller's agent. The agents at Chapter3 have sold on the Grand Strand for decades and hear about these houses before they reach the portals.
+- A reason to be cheap. It pulls every house for sale that has one: a distressed sale, a foreclosure , a short sale, or an older house at the low end of the price range.
 
-Funding the flip
+- The nearby sales. For the cheapest houses per square foot, it finds houses of about the same size that sold within a mile in the last year.
 
-## Fix and flip loans in Myrtle Beach
-Most flips use a hard money loan. It is a short-term loan that closes quickly and can pay for part of the repairs. The rate is higher than a normal mortgage, so every extra month you own the house costs you money.
-You do not have to shop for that loan. Our preferred lender, BrickWood Mortgage , offers fix-and-flip and bridge loans, so the purchase, the loan, and the repair budget are all planned together, by people who have priced Grand Strand rehabs before.
-BrickWood Mortgage also finances new construction for investors. On their investor program they underwrite the deal rather than your income, and in most cases they do not ask for income documents. The guideline they have described to us is that the finished house works as a rental or sells for at least 10 percent above what it cost to build. BrickWood sets those terms and changes them, so ask them what applies to your build. A construction loan on a house you will live in is a different product with more steps.
-Plan how you will sell before you buy. Homes here sell best in spring and summer, so try to finish the project before that season starts. Keep a backup plan too. If the house would make a good rental, refinance into a DSCR loan , which is approved on the property's rent instead of your tax returns, and keep it instead of selling. That approach is called BRRRR : buy, fix, rent, refinance, repeat.
-Before you write the offer, run the address through the analyzer below to see if the rent would cover the house.
