-Paying cash
+- The agent's call. An agent reads the sales, decides what the house will sell for once it is fixed, and works out your highest offer. That number comes from an agent who knows the streets, never from a computer.
 
-## Can I get good opportunities without being a cash buyer?
-A cash offer is stronger than a financed one, and nothing changes that. Bank-owned and off-market listings are the first two places our agents look, and both usually go to the buyer who can close soonest. Cash has no appraisal to fail and no loan to fall through, which those sellers value above a slightly higher price.
-Cash also removes the largest holding cost. No loan interest builds up while the work runs, so a project that slips a month costs you time instead of money. The tradeoff is that your capital sits in one house until it sells, where a construction loan can spread the same cash across several projects at once.
-You can still buy these houses with a loan, and off-market is where it happens, because there is no competing cash offer to lose to. Our agents talk to owners before a house is listed and find out what the seller needs. Then we build the offer around it: the closing date they want, a shorter inspection window, more earnest money, or a price that pays them for waiting on your loan. A seller who has not listed yet is usually weighing one offer instead of five, and that is the situation where financing wins.
+A house far under its neighbors is not always a deal. Sometimes the size in the listing is wrong, the price is an auction's opening bid, or the house needs everything. The finder marks those, and the agent finds out which it is before you spend a dollar.
